/target
# Scratch build directories. `CARGO_TARGET_DIR=target-<something>` is the way to
# build a baseline while another process holds the real `target`, and a few of
# these were created that way during the differential work. They are build
# output, not source: nothing in the repository reads them, and one of them was
# committed by accident, which is what this rule exists to stop happening again.
/target-*/
*.db
*.db-wal
*.db-shm
*.db-journal
test/interop/*.db
# Vendored upstream checkouts and generated fixtures: fetched by tools/, not
# committed.
/test/sqlite-suite/
/testsuite/
/.research/

# Scratch probes left by tooling runs; the tests are the durable record.
**/zzprobe.rs
parseprobe.log
crates/nsqlite/parseprobe.log
test/sqlite-run/

# Per-run scratch from the suite runner and the agents that drive it.
test/sqlite-run/
*.tcl.out

# The suite shim runs a test file in the working directory; stray copies of
# the suite's own .test files land here.
*.test
*.tcl

# Scratch an agent created by redirecting inside a quoted string: a command
# like `sqlite3 > "VACUUM;"` leaves a file whose name is the text between the
# quotes, so these are SQL fragments and shell options rather than anything a
# person meant to create. Matched by pattern rather than by name, because the
# names are whatever statement was in the redirect.
c.db
d.db
e.db
e2e2.db
final.db
nsql_e2e.db
e2e2.db
g.db
pg.db
pg2.db

# SQL fragments and shell options that a redirect inside a quoted string
# created as file names.
/NOSUCHWORD*
/REINDEX*
/SELECT *
/VACUUM*
/ANALYZE*
/ATTACH *
# One rule rather than five. The shell writes a file named after whatever
# followed a redirect, so the set of possible names is whatever the shell
# accepted, and /--* only caught the two-dash ones: `sqlite3 -header -list`
# left `-header` and `-list`, both of them SQLite databases, and both of them
# tracked. A file at the top level whose name starts with a dash is one of
# these -- nothing the tree wants to keep is named that way.
/-*

# Scratch databases the ad-hoc checks leave behind.
tmp/

# Scratch directories and files the differential and suite harnesses write
# while measuring. Each one names a specific measurement rather than anything
# the engine reads, and committing them buries the source changes: a sweep
# script's generated corpus alone is 188k lines.
/tmpwork/
/tmp_parsecheck/
/verify-tmp/
/target-*/
/result.tsv
/*.sql

# A whole runner script, dumped to the top level by a redirect while a
# differential pass was being worked out. It is the third-generation runner
# verbatim -- the one that lives at tools/difftest3.sh -- so tracking it gave
# two copies of the same 84KB and a second place to forget to update.
/src_body.sh
